          %h2#Idea Idea
          %h3#basic_idea Basic idea
          %p
            In the general sense,
            %em superalgebra
            is the study of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+algebra"}> higher
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internalization"} internal
              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
              of
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ℤ
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}_2
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+vector+spaces"} graded vector spaces
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+vector+spaces"}> super vector spaces
              );
          %p equivalently
          %ul
            %li
              over the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+topos"} base topos
              on
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superpoints"} superpoints
          %p
            More specifically, a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supercommutative+superalgebra"} supercommutative superalgebra
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+algebra"} commutative algebra
            in the context of superalgebra.
          %p
            See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+superalgebra"} geometry of physics – superalgebra
            for more on this.
          %p In the following we first discuss
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#AssociativeSuperalgebras"} Associative superalgebras
          %p
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoids"} monoids
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+vector+spaces"} super vector spaces
          %ul
            %li
              %em
                %a{:href => "#AlgebraOverSuperpoints"} Algebra in the topos over superpoints
          %p
            and consider systematically
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+topos"} sheaf topos
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superpoints"} superpoints
            and show how this reproduces and generalizes the previous notions.
          %p
            See (
            %a{:href => "#Sachse"}> Sachse
            ) and the references at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} super ∞-groupoid
            for some history of the topos-theoretic perspective on superalgebra.
          %h3#AbstractIdea Abstract idea
          %p
            We discuss the general abstract
            %em raison d’ être
            of super algebra. Readers looking for just the plain definition should probably
            %a{:href => "#AssociativeSuperalgebras"} skip to below
            on first reading.
          %p
            One way to understand the relevance of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supercommutative+superalgebra"} supercommutative superalgebra
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne%27s+theorem+on+tensor+categories"} Deligne's theorem on tensor categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+categories"} tensor categories
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+representations"} categories of representations
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergroups"} supergroups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braided+monoidal+category"} braiding
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+categories"} tensor categories
          %p
            Superalgebra is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal
            in the following sense. The crucial super-grading rule (the “Koszul sign rule”,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Grassmann1844"} Grassmann 1844, §37, §55
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mn 1
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi deg
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi deg
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi b
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  a \otimes b = (-1)^{deg(a) deg(b)} b \otimes a
          %p
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+vector+spaces"} graded vector spaces
            is induced from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcategory"} subcategory
            which is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+2-group"} abelian 2-group
            of metric graded
            = succeed "." do
